  #block(breakable: false)[
  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A gene is a length of DNA that codes for one characteristic or protein; chromosomes are the structures that carry the genes.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A human somatic cell is diploid with 46 chromosomes — 23 homologous pairs.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Haploid (n) means one set: a human gamete has 23 chromosomes.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Sperm are gametes, so they carry the haploid number, 23.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Homologous chromosomes form a pair, one from each parent, and carry the same genes at the same loci.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The centromere holds the sister chromatids together and is where spindle fibres attach.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    DNA carries the code; the sequence of its bases is the information.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    DNA uses thymine; uracil replaces it in RNA.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A pairs with T by two hydrogen bonds; G pairs with C by three.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A codon is a triplet of bases that specifies one amino acid; the anticodon is its partner on tRNA.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Replication is semi-conservative: each strand acts as a template for a new strand.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Transfer RNA carries a specific amino acid and matches its anticodon to the codon on mRNA.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Gametes are formed by meiosis; mitosis produces genetically identical diploid cells.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Mitosis preserves the chromosome number and the genotype.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Homologous chromosomes pair and exchange segments in prophase I, which is why meiosis produces variation.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    In anaphase I the homologues are pulled to opposite poles, halving the chromosome number.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Meiosis has two divisions, so one diploid cell gives four haploid cells.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The two alleles of a gene separate when gametes form, so each gamete carries only one.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Genes on different chromosome pairs are sorted independently of one another.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Two different alleles of one gene make the organism heterozygous.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Its effect is masked by a dominant allele, so it shows only when both alleles are recessive.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The phenotype is what you can see or measure; the genotype is the alleles that produce it.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Two X chromosomes make a female; an X and a Y make a male.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The egg always carries X; the sperm may carry X or Y, so the father decides.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The chromosomes are arrested in mitosis, stained, photographed and arranged by size and shape.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A dominant allele masks the recessive partner in a heterozygote.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Alleles are the different versions of one gene that occupy the same locus on homologous chromosomes.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Replication happens in interphase, before the cell divides, so each chromosome enters division with two chromatids.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Two haploid gametes fuse in fertilisation, restoring the diploid number.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Because they come from one zygote, identical twins have the same genotype.
  ]

]

